icularly  likely to occur in a market economy onto  the Jews one of the people who did that  somewhat to my surprise it was at least  I was quite surprised by how significant  this was in his thought was Voltaire  who's very striking not only because he  almost single-handedly creates the  modern role of the intellectual but he  somebody who himself was involved in  very wide range of financial activity  including money lending lending money to  royalty in return for annuities trading  in a number of transnational  corporations at the time and we know  from some of those around him that he  often did so in a ravish in a very shady  fashion and indeed as Madame the knee  his longtime mistress said your heart is  pierced by avarice but we tried  keep this hidden well Voltaire responded  by attributing these notions of avarice  and so on precisely to the Jews and he  did so not just not only when he had  contact with Jews but before he had  contact with Jews and in his writings  they occur in an almost a racial sense  that is he he he uses cases cases from  the Bible to project these qualities  back upon Abraham and the patriarchs and  so on so it precedes the late nineteenth  and early twentieth century it's true  that in the late nineteenth and early  twentieth century well first of all the  aristocracy doesn't lose power as  quickly as you might think and some of  them maintain power because they engage  or have relations with new forms of  capitalist activity but it's striking  that one of the one of the major groups  leadership groups behind anti-semitic  movements in say Hungary in the 1890s is  precisely the declining gentry that is  those landowners who are going downhill  because their their economic mode is  really on the decline and they actually  become leaders of the anti-semitic  movement there and you have some similar  cases in Germany it's most striking in  the case of Hungary and yes it's true  that the the you have this interlinking  of criticisms of